# {{.Module}}

Optional modules can be installed with `go tool pfw add auth`. Auth can also be
included at creation with `pfw new -template api -auth`. See `internal/auth/README.md`
after installation for the application principal and security composition point.

Hexagonal API starter using Palma with the {{.Router}} router. Requires Go 1.26 or newer.

The router was selected when creating the project. HTTP-specific code lives in
`internal/item/infrastructure/http` and `internal/platform/http`; the domain,
use cases and repository port are independent of the router.

```sh
go mod tidy
cp env/.env.example env/.env
go tool pfw run -env {{.Environment}}
```

```sh
curl http://localhost:8080/health
curl -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"First item"}' http://localhost:8080/items
curl http://localhost:8080/items
curl http://localhost:8080/items/1
```

The composition declares three environments:

| Environment | Repository | Configuration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `local` | In memory; no SQL pool is opened | `env/.env.local` |
| `staging` | PostgreSQL | `env/.env.staging` |
| `prod` | PostgreSQL | `env/.env.prod` |

Local is the default when creating an API project. `-env` chooses the initial
runtime/generation default from these three names. Data in local is lost on
restart; staging and prod persist items in their respective databases.

## Environment configuration

Dotenv files live in `env/`. `pfw.toml` sets `env_dir = "./env"` for the CLI;
the application loader defaults to the same directory when run directly from
the module root. Override it with `-env-dir` or `PFW_ENV_DIR`.

Copy `env/.env.example` to `env/.env` for common settings. The loader applies `env/.env`,
`env/.env.<environment>` and `env/.env.<environment>.local` in that order; process
variables take precedence over every file. The generated environment files
contain defaults and blank DSNs. They intentionally override any shared DSN so
staging and prod each require an explicit database configuration.

Supply `APP_DB_DSN` through process variables or an ignored override file:

```dotenv
# env/.env.staging.local
APP_DB_DSN=postgres://user:password@staging-host:5432/app
```

Configure production separately in `env/.env.prod.local` or its process environment.
The initial pool limits are 10 open / 2 idle connections in staging and 40 open /
10 idle in prod; adjust them to your database capacity. `env/.env.example` documents
all timeout and pool settings. The shared `env/.env` and private override files are
ignored by Git; `env/.env.local`, `env/.env.staging` and `env/.env.prod` are tracked defaults.

Before starting staging or prod, apply the bundled migration explicitly to the
selected primary database:

```sh
go tool pfw migrate status -env staging
go tool pfw migrate up -env staging
go tool pfw migrate up -env prod
```

Then select the environment, which also regenerates its wiring:

```sh
go tool pfw run -env local
go tool pfw run -env staging
go tool pfw run -env prod
```

A missing DSN in staging/prod fails configuration before opening pools or HTTP.

## Database connection

`internal/item/infrastructure/postgres.Store` already implements
`application.Repository` and receives `MainDB` through its constructor.
`internal/platform/database.OpenMainDB` opens the SQL pools; `BuildWithCleanup`
passes ownership to the lifecycle. Repositories must not close the pools.
The bundled driver is PostgreSQL pgx. Using another SQL database requires
adapting the repository SQL as well as registering its driver in the provider.

`db.Primary()` returns the primary `*sql.DB`. Optionally set `APP_DB_REPLICA_DSN`
to configure a replica named `report`; use `db.Replica("report")` to select it at
runtime. Selection is explicit: a missing replica returns an error, with no
automatic fallback. The starter applies the same driver, timeout and pool limits
to both endpoints; edit the provider for independent settings, additional named
replicas or separate database groups with distinct Go types.

Queries use the driver's normal API. To participate in an application transaction,
use `sqltx.Executor(ctx, db.Primary())` from
`github.com/palma99/palma-framework/transaction/sql`. Selecting a replica inside
a transaction on the primary returns an error. No ORM or query builder is required. Migrations are managed explicitly by the
CLI; connection setup does not create the application's tables.

The initializer receives the startup context, so SIGINT/SIGTERM also cancel a
database connection attempt. Pool cleanup runs after the HTTP server stops and
also runs if a later constructor fails.

## Migrations

The starter includes `migrations/000001_create_items.sql` with `-- +pfw Up` and
`-- +pfw Down` sections.
`pfw.toml` sets `migrations_dir = "./migrations"`. Create the next file with
`go tool pfw migrate create add_fields`, then write the SQL before applying it.
The primary DSN and environment configuration are shared with the application.

```sh
go tool pfw migrate up -env staging       # all pending versions
go tool pfw migrate status -env staging
go tool pfw migrate down -env staging     # undo one version
```

`-steps N` limits up/down. The PostgreSQL tracking table records checksums;
keep applied files unchanged and add a new version to change the schema.
Each migration runs in a transaction under a database advisory lock. SQL that
requires execution outside a transaction is not supported. Rollback executes
exactly the SQL written in the `Down` section and can remove data. Migrations do not
run automatically during API startup. For another database, use `-dsn-env` and
`-dir` to select its primary DSN variable and migration directory.

## Architecture

- `internal/item/domain`: entities and invariants; no framework or transport imports.
- `internal/item/application`: use cases and output port; depends on the domain.
- `internal/item/infrastructure/http`: HTTP infrastructure, DTOs, JSON decoding and error mapping.
- `internal/item/infrastructure/memory`: concurrency-safe local storage.
- `internal/item/infrastructure/postgres`: persistent SQL repository for staging/prod.
- `migrations`: versioned SQL schema changes.
- `env`: environment defaults and private dotenv overrides.
- `internal/config`: typed configuration with validation and dotenv loading.
- `internal/platform/http`: router and server configuration.
- `internal/platform/database`: typed `MainDB` provider, SQL pools and optional replica.
- `internal/bootstrap`: composition root and generated dependency wiring.
- `cmd/api`: startup and graceful shutdown through the Palma lifecycle.

## Wiring and environments

The composition uses `pfw.Discover("../...")` to discover providers throughout
`internal`. Mark each dependency constructor with `//pfw:coconut`, immediately
above its declaration. The starter providers already have these annotations;
there is no constructor list to maintain. `pfw.AutoBind()` is enabled at the build
root: interfaces with one compatible provider are selected automatically.
Modules inherit this setting unless they declare their own `pfw.AutoBind()` or
`pfw.AutoBind(false)`. With multiple candidates, add an explicit `pfw.Bind`.
Domain factories and other helpers do not need annotations.

`NewErrorMapper` is an annotated provider in
`internal/item/infrastructure/http/error_mapper.go`. The HTTP composition receives
`pfwhttp.ErrorMapper` through DI; autobinding selects the discovered `*pfwhttp.Mapper`.
Change its rules in the provider, or add a custom mapper and an explicit binding
when multiple compatible implementations are discovered.

The server logs `server started` with the bound address and environment after
successfully opening its listener. Startup errors do not emit a readiness log.
The framework HTTP lifecycle component is used with both stdlib and Echo routers.

The HTTP error boundary and the lifecycle component receive `logging.Logger` through DI.
Palma supplies the standard logger and HTTP lifecycle component as fallback
providers. There is no application logger factory or lifecycle wrapper to maintain.

To customize the standard logger, add an annotated constructor returning
`*logging.SlogLogger`. To choose a different implementation, add its annotated
constructor and `pfw.Bind[logging.Logger, *custom.Logger]()` to the composition.
An initializer input or a provider returning `logging.Logger` directly also
replaces the fallback. `pfw.ForEnv` can select a logger for a declared environment.
Application ambiguities and missing explicitly bound providers remain errors.

The main reports bootstrap errors on stderr and does not create another logger.

```sh
go generate ./internal/bootstrap
go tool pfw generate -env {{.Environment}} -check
PFW_ENV={{.Environment}} go test ./...
go build -o bin/api ./cmd/api
PFW_ENV={{.Environment}} ./bin/api
```

The CLI is pinned through the `tool` directive in go.mod. A local framework
replacement requires that checkout; replace it with a published version when
sharing the project independently.

The bindings select Postgres by default and override it with memory through
`pfw.ForEnv("local", ...)`. These rules belong to this starter's composition;
the framework itself does not infer storage from environment names. Generate
wiring again before building for a different environment. The binary rejects
a runtime environment that differs from its generated graph.

The generated tests use `PFW_ENV` (default `local`) to match the selected graph.
They exercise the actual SQL repository with a local test driver in staging/prod,
without connecting to external databases.

`pfw.toml` configures the bootstrap and main package paths, relative to the module
root. `go tool pfw generate`, `inspect` and `run` use these paths when no package
is supplied; explicit package arguments override them.

## Controllers and middleware

`internal/item/infrastructure/http/controller.go` owns local endpoints (`List`,
`Get`, `Create`). `internal/platform/http/server.go` mounts them at `/items`.
Move the mount to `/api/v1/items` to version the API; creation responses use
that mount in their `Location` header.

{{if eq .Router "echo"}}`Controller.Register` receives an `*echo.Group`. Use native group middleware at
the mount, or pass `echo.MiddlewareFunc` values to individual `GET`/`POST`
registrations. Handlers return errors; the router's `HTTPErrorHandler` applies
the injected application mapper. Native Echo routing and middleware errors
retain their status and error handling.
{{else}}`Controller.Register` receives a mux, a prefix and optional middleware of type
`func(http.Handler) http.Handler`. Middleware values run in declaration order.
Handlers return errors and use `pfwhttp.HandlerAdapter.Wrap` to become native
`http.HandlerFunc` values. Wrap individual endpoints in `Register` to apply
route-specific middleware, or wrap the server's mux for global middleware.
{{end}}
Middleware components can receive services through DI. Adding a controller
requires an explicit mount in the HTTP composition; provider discovery does
not expose routes automatically.
